e  sense of what happened and make sense of  why I was made to feel like I was to  blame so the kinds of  overwhelming changes are happening and  the events that were happening in my  life at the time and so first it became  a way to make sense of my own experience  and that's why it became so personal to  me and then once I've made that entry  point I then started to realize that  it's not that women's rights aren't  siphoned off from the rest of society it  was almost like a snowballing effect I  started with feminism and then I  realized women can't be free until you  know we defeat race oppression until we  fight class oppression because all of  these things are so intimately tied so  it was the entry point of politics for  me and has undergirded everything since  then my entry into politics was  emotional  before it was intellectual and that's  not something that I shy away from I  think that that is something I'm very  proud of but it's definitely something  that through explaining I've managed to  process the kind of various ways that  gender oppression race oppression  feature in my own life were you then as  influenced as much as much by the men  who were in your like men and boys who  were in your life during that time as  the feminist literature itself so I went  to an all-girls school you know for a  lot of my schooling and then in my last  two years of schooling I went to  actually an all-boys school that took in  a very small number of women in their  sick form so that was quite a formative  experience in me and seeing how a lot of  the masculine ways of forming social  bonds can be harmful to men it became  really clear to me that men didn't know  how to form relationships with women in  a way that was conducive for both their  happiness and healthiness  and women's happiness and healthiness  but I also think we do need to sometimes  shift away from dividing people into men  and women and look at people as being  sort of feminists or patriarchs because  you can have women who behave in a  patriarchal manner and pursue a  patriarchal system and men who are very  feminist in their orientation in terms  of where we are right now and of course  you one can't one can't generalize about  feminism as a whole but am I wrong to  detect that there are parts of  feminism these days that are that are  eyes angry about things that are going  on justifiably so in many many cases as  much as they have ever been perhaps  since the sixties the the level of anger  is it on occasion is comparable to back  then yes I think we saw a massive dip  right in the kind of naughties where  feminism really became a dirty words I  remember being the only only person  who's self-described as a feminist and  that all-boys school that I was talking  about and what happened how did that how  do people respond to that well it wasn't  easy and I think I faced a lot of  hostility not just from male students  but also from other female students it  was a difficult expect and I definitely  felt very alone now I think we're seeing  almost the opposites in that companies  and institutions are scrambling to be  seen as feminists and I think whilst  this has definitely created a space for  women to raise grievances and and for  kind of gendered oppression to be put  into the spotlight I think in some ways  it can be a poisoned chalice because it  can be very contradictory you look at  night for example who recently did a  campaign I think it was called look what  she's made of which was kind of about  again like appropriating that idea of  feminism or appropriating an aesthetic  of feminism but then you look at their  supply chains just last month 350 women  in their sweatshops were hospitalized  because of extreme fatigue from poor  working conditions so whilst I think  that it's definitely a plus that  feminism is not a dirty word anymore I  think what I don't want to see is a  defamed feminism that doesn't look at  the structural conditions that affect  women as a whole you know and  particularly marginalized and  working-class women as well I think  we're looking rather than looking to  rewire the system we're looking at sort  of getting women to play by the rules of  the game but the rules of the game are  rigged against the majority of women you  know at the moment you've got I stand  with her  you have the me2 movement in  International Women's Day is a feature  of any major organizations calendar now  taken together it doesn't that represent  significant or more than significant  progress I don't think we can look at it  as progress because when you look at the  in terms of I don't think it's a static  line sort of going up because if you  look at the first International Women's  Day that to me was one of the most  militant moments in feminist history you  had you know women garment workers  migrant women garment workers in New  York going on strike to demand better  conditions that was a collective demand  to change the collective experience of  women and to change the conditions of  the most vulnerable and the most  exploited women to me that's more  progress than a kind of feminism that  focuses on putting a female head at the  top of a system that entrenches  inequality seventy percent of the  world's poor are women so if you  perpetuate a system that increases that  inequality women are going to be at the  sharp edge of that I mean a woman you  could perhaps have have included in that  description I mean Hillary Clinton so  much of her campaign to become president  was about having a woman as as the first  president I mean didn't she have a point  I mean wouldn't that have been more than  us like again more than a significant  step forward for there to be the first  female president of the United States  so representation is obviously important  it's obviously important to see people  that look like you being celebrated and  being revered right or being trusted  with that kind of decision-making power  but I don't think the representation is  a replacement for systemic change when  you look at Hillary Clinton specifically  and you look at for example the fact  that she was completely embedded with  the fossil fuel industry you know the  fossil fuel industry contributed  millions of dollars to her campaign when  you look at the impacts of climate  change in the impact of the fossil fuel  industries extractivism globally eighty  percent of the people that are displaced  by climate change are women we know that  women are the most affected by climate  change so to me that's a massive  contradiction and I don't think that  it's a fair payoff a lot of what you're  saying seems to go to something that  you've talked about in your writing the  idea that feminine I don't know P words  in your mouth but the idea that feminism  might be incompatible with capitalism a  system that has inequality at its heart  I fundamentally don't think can be  compatible  with an ideology with a politics that  fights for liberation for all I don't  think it's an accident that 70% of the  world's poor are women so for me  socialism and feminism or rewiring our  economic system to be more equal and  more based in care and more based in  justice is intimately tied with gender  equality and ending gender oppression in  terms of where feminism sits at the  moment at times it does whether there  are voices within the feminist movement  that seem to have an issue with with  with trans with trans politics as well  do you understand where where some of  these people are coming from and are on  that issue there's something being lost  from the notion of what it is to be a  woman I absolutely don't think that  we're seeing the most privileged sector  of women trying to just trying to police  the boundaries of womanhood in order to  maintain some kind of power within this  movement trans politics is inherently  feminist politics because it challenges  the primp the gender binary and that's  fundamentally what feminism has always  done so what what then is the rule of  men in this in this struggle I mean can  a man be a feminist can someone without  the experiences that you have had be a  feminist truly I completely believe they  can of course because patriarchy is a  system that yes it benefits men more  than it benefits women but it still does  hurt men because it's a system that is  based on competition on violence on  oppression on hierarchy and that is  something that effects men as well as  women but women do obviously suffer the  brunt of it can you understand why there  are some men who feel perhaps rightly  perhaps wrongly that for women to rise  someone else has to be brought down well  I think that that's symptomatic of the  way that our economic and political  system is set up it's set up on the  premise of hierarchy on the premise that  it's okay I think it's the top 27  richest people in the world owning more  wealth than the bottom 50% so when you  have a system that's been  based on hierarchy and based on the  incredible riches of some and the  incredible poverty of the many then of  course you would think oh if for some  people to be liberated other people need  to be put down but when you rewire the  entire way that you envisage society you  can see that actually our liberation is  completely tied together and that's why  for me the Hillary Clinton style  feminism the Teresa may style feminism  that focuses on the progression of  individual very privileged women is not  interesting to me because it doesn't  capture that radical potential that  feminism has to change society for  everyone I have no interest in leaning  into a system that has inequality at its  heart and I have no interest in smashing  a glass ceiling if it means that the  majority of the web of poor women are  cleaning up the shards of that glass  ceiling

